## 2. Quantitative Comparative Matrix (Theoretical Comparisons)

### 2.1 Comparative Framework Synthesis

| Dimension / Feature | Kurzweil Law of Accelerating Returns | Perez Techno-Economic Paradigms | Kondratiev Long Waves | Bresnahan & Trajtenberg GPT Model | Nordhaus Neoclassical Growth |
| :----- | :------ | :--- | :---- | :----- | :------ |
| **Primary Driver** | Information feedback loops & substrate shift | Financial vs production capital investment | Technological innovation clustering | Downstream innovation spillovers | Total Factor Productivity ($A$) & capital $K$ |
| **Mathematical Model** | $P(t) = P_0 \cdot 2^{2^{k \cdot t}}$ | Logistic S-curve per paradigm | Harmonic long wave ($\sim 50$) | Complementary R&D spillover functions | Cobb-Douglas: $Y = A K^\alpha L^{1-\alpha}$ |
| **Role of Crises** | Temporary friction along envelope | Structural turning point between phases | Trough of long wave economic depression | Coordination failures across industries | Exogenous productivity shocks |
| **Paradigm Shift Mechanism** | Substrate saturation ($P_i \rightarrow K_i$) | Institutional realignment post-crash | Capital reinvestment in new sectors | Vertical & horizontal R&D integration | Capital deepening & factor substitution |
| **Current Horizon State** | Epoch 4 $\rightarrow$ Epoch 5 Transition | 5th ICT Deployment / 6th Installation | 6th Kondratiev Wave (AI/Biotech) | Digital Compute & Deep AI GPT Spillover | TFP Growth Bounded ($\sim 1.5\%$) |


## 3. Physical Limits, Architectural Constraints & Hardware Barriers (Theoretical Comparisons)

### 3.1 Physics Governing Theoretical Synthesis
All macro theories must satisfy physical constraints at the hardware layer (Hennessy & Patterson, 2017):

1. **Dennard Scaling Breakdown (2005) (Theoretical Comparisons & Macro-Historical Synthesis Monograph)**: Sub-threshold leakage ($I_{} \propto \exp((V_{gs}-V_{th})/nV_T)$) ended frequency scaling ($P = C V_{dd}^2 f$), marking the physical boundary between Perez's 5th ICT Installation phase and the multi-core GPU era (Dennard et al., 1974).
2. **Thermodynamic Landauer Bound**: Heat dissipation per bit erasure ($E_{} = k_B T \ln 2 \approx 2.87 \times 10^{-21}$ at $300$) limits the physical density of compute substrates, framing the thermodynamic limits of Schumpeterian hardware replacement (Landauer, 1961).
3. **Amdahl's Law vs. Gustafson's Weak Scaling (Theoretical Comparisons & Macro-Historical Synthesis Monograph)**: Amdahl's serial limit ($S_{} = \frac{1}{(1-p) + p/s}$) and Gustafson's weak scaling ($S_{} = (1-p) + p \cdot s$) determine whether AI operates as a macroeconomic General Purpose Technology with linear parallel scalability (Amdahl, 1967; Gustafson, 1988).


## 4. Strategic Implications & Theoretical Synthesis (2026–2035+) (Theoretical Comparisons) (Theoretical Comparisons Deep Dive)
Synthesizing LoAR with Perez, Kondratiev, and Schumpeterian frameworks reveals that while compute hardware capability expands double-exponentially, macroeconomic transformation is governed by institutional deployment cycles. Between 2026 and 2035, the 6th Kondratiev wave (AI, synthetic biology, clean energy) will drive massive capital reallocation, transforming the global economy as artificial intelligence matures into the ultimate General Purpose Technology.


